👽### Executable files and formats that can lead to Remote Code Execution (RCE)

Remote Code Execution (RCE) occurs when an attacker can cause a vulnerable application, server, device, or runtime to execute commands or code remotely.

Important: A file format does not automatically create an RCE vulnerability. The risk depends on how the target processes, loads, deserializes, interprets, or executes that file.

For offensive security research, classify the files into these groups.

### 1\. Native executable files

These can contain machine instructions executed directly by a compatible operating system or loaded by another program.

| Format         | Typical platform | Security relevance                             |
| -------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| .exe, .dll     | Windows          | Executable and loadable PE files               |
| .elf, .so      | Linux/Android    | Executables and shared libraries               |
| .dylib, Mach-O | macOS/iOS        | Executables and dynamic libraries              |
| .bin           | Embedded/Linux   | May contain raw machine code or firmware       |
| .sys           | Windows          | Kernel drivers                                 |
| .ko            | Linux            | Kernel modules                                 |
| .o, .obj       | Multiple         | Object files, normally linked before execution |

### 2\. Script files

These can execute when passed to a compatible interpreter or runtime.

| Format          | Interpreter/runtime         |
| --------------- | --------------------------- |
| .sh, .bash      | Bash or another shell       |
| .py             | Python                      |
| .js, .mjs, .cjs | JavaScript runtime          |
| .php            | PHP                         |
| .rb             | Ruby                        |
| .pl             | Perl                        |
| .lua            | Lua                         |
| .ps1            | PowerShell                  |
| .bat, .cmd      | Windows command interpreter |
| .vbs, .vbe      | Windows Script Host         |
| .tcl            | Tcl                         |
| .awk            | AWK                         |
| .groovy         | Groovy                      |
| .kts            | Kotlin scripting runtime    |

### 3\. Bytecode and virtual-machine files

These contain instructions for a virtual machine or language runtime.

| Format                | Runtime                          |
| --------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| .class, .jar          | Java Virtual Machine             |
| .war, .ear            | Java application servers         |
| .dex                  | Android Runtime                  |
| .odex, .vdex, .oat    | Android runtime artifacts        |
| .pyc                  | Python                           |
| .wasm                 | WebAssembly runtime              |
| .beam                 | Erlang/Elixir VM                 |
| .luac                 | Lua VM                           |
| .pyo                  | Historical Python bytecode       |
| .cubin, .ptx          | NVIDIA GPU execution environment |
| .spv                  | SPIR-V-compatible runtime        |
| .eBPF / eBPF bytecode | Linux eBPF virtual machine       |

### 4\. Archives and packages that can contain executable code

These are especially important because the outer file may look like ordinary data while containing executable components.

| Format             | Possible executable contents                            |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| .apk               | DEX, ELF .so, scripts and assets                        |
| .aab               | Android application modules and executable code         |
| .apex              | Android system components                               |
| .zip               | Scripts, libraries, plugins or application packages     |
| .jar               | Java classes and resources                              |
| .war               | Java web application                                    |
| .ear               | Enterprise Java application                             |
| .phar              | PHP application archive                                 |
| .whl               | Python package                                          |
| .egg               | Python package                                          |
| .gem               | Ruby package                                            |
| .deb               | Linux package with executables and installation scripts |
| .rpm               | Linux package with executables and scripts              |
| .msi               | Windows installer                                       |
| .cab               | Windows cabinet archive                                 |
| .7z, .rar, .tar.gz | Archives that may contain executable files              |

### 5\. Web application executable extensions

These are particularly important when testing upload functionality on websites.

| Technology             | Extensions to recognize                               |
| ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| PHP                    | .php, .php3, .php4, .php5, .php7, .phtml, .phar, .pht |
| ASP                    | .asp, .asa, .asax, .ascx, .ashx, .asmx                |
| ASP.NET                | .aspx, .ashx, .asmx, .ascx                            |
| Java web               | .jsp, .jspx, .jspf, .jsw, .jsv                        |
| Python                 | .py, .pyc, .pyw                                       |
| Ruby                   | .rb, .rbw                                             |
| Perl                   | .pl, .pm, .cgi                                        |
| Shell                  | .sh, .bash, .cgi                                      |
| Server-side JavaScript | .js, .mjs, .cjs                                       |
| Server configuration   | .htaccess, .user.ini, web.config                      |

These extensions are only relevant if the server has a matching interpreter, handler, plugin, or configuration that processes them. A `.php` file stored as a download does not automatically execute. OWASP specifically identifies upload location and server execution configuration as important conditions. GitHub +1

### 6\. Files that may trigger RCE through vulnerable processing

This category is easy to overlook. The file itself may be data, not executable code, but a vulnerable parser or converter can turn processing it into code execution.

| File type          | Extension examples                          | Potentially vulnerable component               |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Images             | .jpg, .jpeg, .png, .gif, .webp, .tiff, .svg | Image decoders, converters, SVG/XML processors |
| Documents          | .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .rtf               | Document viewers and conversion software       |
| Office             | .xls, .xlsx, .ppt, .pptx, .docm, .xlsm      | Office applications, macro engines, previewers |
| Archives           | .zip, .tar, .gz, .7z, .rar                  | Extraction libraries and archive scanners      |
| Audio              | .mp3, .wav, .flac, .ogg, .m4a               | Audio decoders                                 |
| Video              | .mp4, .avi, .mkv, .mov, .webm               | Video codecs and transcoding services          |
| Fonts              | .ttf, .otf, .woff, .woff2                   | Font parsers and rendering engines             |
| XML                | .xml, .xhtml, .svg                          | XML parsers and external-entity processing     |
| Database           | .db, .sqlite, .sql                          | Database engines, importers and extensions     |
| Serialized objects | .ser, .pkl, .pickle, .marshal, .bin         | Object deserialization libraries               |

Key distinction: A malformed image, PDF, archive or audio file is not automatically an RCE. RCE requires a flaw in the software processing it, or an unsafe configuration or execution path. Insecure deserialization is another important route. OWASP Web Security Testing Guide +2

### 7\. Android APK-specific executable components

For Android application security research, prioritize:

| File/component    | What to investigate                             |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| classes.dex       | Application bytecode and unsafe execution paths |
| classes2.dex etc. | Additional DEX code                             |
| lib/*.so          | Native code, memory corruption and JNI          |
| .odex, .oat       | Optimized runtime code                          |
| .vdex             | Verified DEX-related runtime data               |
| .wasm             | WebAssembly modules                             |
| .js               | JavaScript execution                            |
| .jar              | Java libraries and code                         |
| .apk              | Package contents and installation behavior      |
| .apex             | Android system components                       |

Look for dangerous combinations such as downloaded code being dynamically loaded, unsafe native libraries, vulnerable third-party SDKs, and exported components that allow untrusted input to reach sensitive code.

### 8\. The most important RCE pathways to investigate

A file becomes an RCE concern when it reaches a vulnerable execution or processing mechanism.

Common RCE pathways

* Unrestricted file upload

    An application accepts a file that can subsequently be executed by the server.

* Unsafe file inclusion

    User-controlled file paths or remote resources are included as executable code.

* Insecure deserialization

    Untrusted serialized objects trigger dangerous methods or gadget chains.

* Vulnerable file parser

    Processing a specially malformed file exploits a flaw in an image, document, archive or media library.

* Unsafe archive extraction

    Archive paths or extracted files overwrite sensitive locations or application code.

* Dynamic code loading

    An application downloads or loads untrusted scripts, libraries or bytecode.

* Command injection

    File names, metadata or file-processing parameters reach an operating-system command unsafely.

OWASP documents these risks in its file-upload, file-inclusion and insecure-deserialization testing guidance. OWASP Web Security Testing Guide +2

### 9\. How to investigate these files as an offensive security researcher

Only test applications and servers you own or have explicit permission to assess.

Step 1 — Identify the actual file format

On Ubuntu:

```bash
file suspicious_file
xxd -l 64 suspicious_file
strings -a suspicious_file | head -50
sha256sum suspicious_file
```

Do not trust the extension alone. Inspect the file's actual content and magic bytes.

Step 2 — Inspect executable structures

For ELF files:

```bash
readelf -h application
readelf -S application
readelf -l application
readelf -d application
readelf -s application
```

For Android:

```bash
unzip -l target.apk
jadx -d jadx_output target.apk
apktool d target.apk -o decoded
find . -type f -name "*.so"
find . -type f -name "*.dex"
```

For WebAssembly:

```bash
wasm-objdump -h module.wasm
wasm-tools print module.wasm
```

Step 3 — Inspect file-processing functionality

Look for:

* Upload endpoints.

* Image resizing and document conversion.

* Archive extraction.

* Import/export functionality.

* Plugin and theme installation.

* Dynamic library loading.

* Deserialization of user-controlled data.

* File paths passed into shell commands.

Step 4 — Validate safely

Use harmless test files and controlled test applications. Confirm whether the file is rejected, stored, parsed, extracted, interpreted or executed. Do not upload a real web shell or destructive payload to a production target.

Step 5 — Establish impact

A successful upload is not proof of RCE. Establish whether untrusted input actually causes server-side code execution, and document the affected component, execution context, reproducibility and impact.

OWASP's testing guidance covers these distinctions and recommended checks. OWASP Web Security Testing Guide +2

### 10\. Your priority list

If your goal is to discover serious RCE vulnerabilities in Android applications and websites, I would study these first:

| Priority | Area                                                  |
| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| 1        | PHP, JSP, ASPX and other server-side script execution |
| 2        | ELF .so and native libraries                          |
| 3        | DEX and Android dynamic code loading                  |
| 4        | ZIP/TAR archive extraction                            |
| 5        | Java, PHP and Python deserialization                  |
| 6        | Image and document processing libraries               |
| 7        | WebAssembly and JavaScript runtimes                   |
| 8        | Installer, plugin and package processing              |
| 9        | XML and SVG parsers                                   |
| 10       | Firmware and embedded executable formats              |

One principle to remember: Don't just ask, "Which executable files does this application accept?" Ask, "Which component processes this file, what privileges does that component have, and can untrusted input influence what it executes?"

That is how you move from identifying executable files to finding genuine RCE vulnerabilities.

